{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon OpenSearch Serverless now adds audit logs for data plane APIs - AWS","context":"Amazon OpenSearch Serverless has introduced support for detailed audit logging of data plane requests through AWS CloudTrail. This feature allows customers to record user actions on their collections, helping them comply with regulations, enhance security, and provide evidence for security investigations. Users can now monitor activities such as authorization attempts, index modifications, and search queries. CloudTrail offers filters for OpenSearch Serverless collections with read-only and write-only options, as well as advanced event selectors for more precise control over logged data events. All OpenSearch Serverless data events are delivered to an Amazon S3 bucket and optionally to Amazon CloudWatch Events, creating a comprehensive audit trail. This new level of visibility into API call activities assists security and operations teams in monitoring data access and reacting to events in real-time. Once configured with CloudTrail, audit logs will be continuously streamed with no additional customer action required","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/11/amazon-opensearch-serverless-auditlogs-dataplane-apis/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Analyze your CloudTrail events using natural language in Amazon Q Console - AWS","context":"AWS CloudTrail, a service used for recording API activity across AWS accounts for security auditing, compliance, and operational troubleshooting, now integrates with Amazon Q Console. This integration enables users to investigate AWS account activity using natural language. Users can ask Amazon Q Console about CloudTrail configuration, query logged events for security investigations, and troubleshoot operational issues without writing queries or manually parsing log files. With this integration, users can check if CloudTrail trails are properly configured, identify gaps in logging coverage, and confirm which data event sources are being tracked. For security concerns, users can ask about who accessed specific IAM roles, changes made to VPC configurations, and unauthorized access attempts. For operational troubleshooting, users can ask about who created or deleted resources, identify API calls generating errors, trace activity from specific IP addresses, or determine why bills spiked. Amazon Q Console can query CloudTrail trails, associated CloudWatch log groups, and event data stores on behalf of users, providing answers grounded in actual account activity. This integration is available in all AWS commercial regions where Amazon Q Console is supported. To get started, users can open Amazon Q in the AWS Management Console and ask questions about their CloudTrail configuration or account activity. For more information, visit the AWS CloudTrail documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/09/cloudtrail-amazon-q-console/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region","skill_description":"Enables a multi-region AWS CloudTrail trail with S3 log storage, CloudWatch Logs integration, and CloudWatch Logs Insights queries for security monitoring and compliance auditing. Use when setting up centralized API activity logging across all AWS regions.\n\nServices: cloudtrail, s3, cloudwatch, iam, kms\nTasks: configure, secure, monitor\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: security, monitoring\n*","skill_name":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"AWS improves regional resiliency for root user sign-in - AWS","context":"AWS has improved the resiliency of root user sign-ins by distributing the traffic across US East (N. Virginia), US East (Ohio), and US West (Oregon) regions. This change reduces the dependence on US East (N. Virginia) and enhances the service's ability to withstand disruptions. The sign-in process is automated, and users do not need to select a region or change their signing-in method. Root user sign-in events will be recorded in CloudTrail based on the Region that processed the request. To maintain complete visibility into root user sign-in activity, users should update their monitoring and alerting systems to cover the aforementioned regions. For more information, refer to the AWS Sign-In documentation and CloudTrail ConsoleLogin event reference","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/09/root-user-regional-resiliency/"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"aws-cloudformation","skill_description":"Authors, validates, and troubleshoots AWS CloudFormation templates. Covers template authoring with secure defaults, local validation with either cfn-lint or cloudformation-validate, cfn-guard security and compliance checks as a recommended default, account-aware CloudFormation service pre-deployment validation, CloudFormation Express mode for faster deployments, and root-cause diagnosis of failed stacks using CloudFormation events and CloudTrail correlation. Also covers author-time template intelligence with the CloudFormation Language Server and published cloudformation-validate libraries.\n\nServices: cloudformation, cloudtrail, iam\nTasks: author, validate, deploy, debug, troubleshoot\nPersona: developer, devops, architect\nWorkload: infrastructure-as-code\n*","skill_name":"aws-cloudformation"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify CloudTrail GetEventSelectors behavior for organization trails from member accounts"}

