{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"AWS Organizations emits CloudTrail events for account membership changes - AWS","context":"AWS Organizations now emits CloudTrail events to the management account whenever accounts join or leave the organization. These new events, AccountJoinedOrganization and AccountDepartedOrganization, provide enhanced visibility into organizational membership changes. The AccountJoinedOrganization event captures how an account joined (Created or Invited) and the join timestamp. The AccountDepartedOrganization event records how an account departed (Left for voluntary departures, Removed for account removal by the management account, or Cleaned for permanently closed accounts) and the departure timestamp. These events can be used to create CloudWatch alarms or Amazon EventBridge rules for real-time notifications, enabling rapid response to suspicious organizational changes. These CloudTrail events are available in all commercial, AWS GovCloud (US), and China Regions","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/05/aws-organizations-cloudtrail/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Uncover blind spots in AWS data plane operations with CloudTrail Event Coverage - AWS","context":"AWS CloudTrail's new feature, Event Coverage, allows users to view their data plane operations coverage at the account and organization levels in the AWS CloudTrail console. This feature helps identify gaps in logging posture by showing which AWS services and resource types have data event logging enabled and which do not. Data events enable tracking of data plane operations in AWS services, such as Amazon S3 object-level operations, to detect unauthorized access or exfiltration attempts. Event Coverage provides a centralized view of coverage across all supported data event sources and enables subscribing to data events directly from the dashboard. This simplifies closing coverage gaps, especially for organizations managing multiple accounts. Event Coverage is available in all commercial AWS Regions where AWS CloudTrail is supported. For more information on logging Data Events, visit the AWS CloudTrail documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/09/aws-cloudtrail-event-coverage/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"AWS CloudTrail launches Insights for data events to automatically detect anomalies in data access - AWS","context":"AWS CloudTrail Insights, a feature that helps identify and respond to unusual activity in AWS accounts, has been expanded to include data events. Previously, Insights analyzed only management events, but now it also monitors data events, enhancing the ability to detect potential security or operational issues. Insights for data events automatically detects anomalies in data access activities, such as increased delete Amazon S3 object API calls or error rates for AWS Lambda function invocations. This feature establishes normal baselines for data access patterns and alerts users when it detects anomalies, providing the relevant data events from the anomaly period for precise investigation. Users can configure alerts for potential issues and respond rapidly to threats or issues. Insights for data events is available in all regions where AWS CloudTrail is available, and additional charges apply for this feature. To get started, users should refer to the AWS CloudTrail documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/11/cloudtrail-insights-data-events-detect-anomalies-access/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region","skill_description":"Enables a multi-region AWS CloudTrail trail with S3 log storage, CloudWatch Logs integration, and CloudWatch Logs Insights queries for security monitoring and compliance auditing. Use when setting up centralized API activity logging across all AWS regions.\n\nServices: cloudtrail, s3, cloudwatch, iam, kms\nTasks: configure, secure, monitor\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: security, monitoring\n*","skill_name":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"querying-aws-s3","skill_description":"Queries S3 object metadata, tracks bucket activity, audits object changes, searches annotations, and analyzes storage metrics using S3 Metadata system tables (journal, inventory, annotation) and S3 Storage Lens tables via Athena SQL. Applies when counting objects, finding recent uploads or deletions, identifying who wrote to a prefix, breaking down storage classes, finding objects by tag, searching annotation content, analyzing storage lens metrics, or enabling S3 Metadata tracking. Prefers system tables over raw S3 APIs (list-objects-v2, head-object) at scale. Trigger phrases: bucket activity, object count, who uploaded, track deletions, storage class breakdown, find by tag, search annotations, storage lens metrics, audit bucket changes.\n\nServices: s3, s3tables, athena, glue\nTasks: debug, audit, configure\nPersona: developer, data-engineer, devops\nWorkload: data-analytics, observability\n*","skill_name":"querying-aws-s3"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify whether organization CloudTrail trails cover member account S3 data events"}
